---
/**
 * Section 04 — Multiple solver paradigms, as a label-plus-content band stack.
 * Follows the CausalStack shape from DESIGN.md §8.4: fixed mono label column,
 * accent tick on the left edge, staggered entrance gated on `.in-view`.
 *
 * The QTT band states storage and runtime separately on purpose. The crate
 * README was truthed up against the immersed harness measurement, which found
 * per-step wall clock rising far faster than the stored size, so a band that
 * said "costs" would be claiming something the measurement contradicts.
 */
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import { rustDark, rustLight } from '../../../shiki-rust-themes.mjs';

const SHIKI = { light: rustLight, dark: rustDark };

const families = [
  {
    key: 'DEC',
    label: 'Calculus',
    title: 'DEC-native Navier–Stokes',
    forWhat: 'Incompressible flow on a fixed mesh.',
    body:
      'Velocity lives as an edge 1-form on a discrete exterior calculus. Each step marches the Leray-projected rate, so the field is divergence-free at every step. The SolenoidalField type-state carries that: there is no public constructor, every constructing path is a projection, and the type implements no arithmetic, so two projected fields cannot be added into an unprojected one.',
    caveat:
      'Two wall-bounded escape hatches are public. constrain_edges and with_lift re-wrap a modified tensor without re-projecting, because the solver re-enters them on the output of a projection that already pinned those edges. Off that path the caller carries the invariant.',
    evidence: [
      { text: 'Ghia cavity Re 1000', href: '/validation/#dec-cavity' },
      { text: 'Cylinder Re 100', href: '/validation/#dec-cylinder' },
      { text: 'Graded MMS, order 2.00', href: '/validation/#dec-graded-mms' },
    ],
  },
  {
    key: 'QTT',
    label: 'Compression',
    title: 'Quantized tensor-train marchers',
    forWhat: 'Compressible Euler, 1-D through 3-D, including a body-fitted variant.',
    body:
      'A 2^L grid stores order χ²·L: logarithmic in point count for a bounded bond dimension χ, with sharp structure paid for in χ. Whether χ stays bounded is the question the rank studies answered, and they found the driver to be coordinate alignment rather than sharpness. The compressible carrier acts on that with a shock-fitted inflow strip, which imposes the exact Rankine–Hugoniot state as the boundary of the marched layer, so the shock is never captured at all.',
    caveat:
      'Storage is not runtime. On the incompressible immersed harness, per-step wall clock rose far faster than χ²·L while the achieved bond stayed flat, so a non-compression bottleneck dominates that path.',
    evidence: [
      { text: 'Sod, L1 ≤ 0.027', href: '/validation/#qtt-sod' },
      { text: 'RAM-C II anchor', href: '/validation/#qtt-ramc' },
      { text: 'Alignment is the rank driver', href: '/boundaries/#alignment' },
    ],
  },
  {
    key: 'Analytic',
    label: 'Pointwise',
    title: 'Fitted closures',
    forWhat: 'Gridless stagnation-line and relaxation problems.',
    body:
      'Exact Rankine–Hugoniot jumps, Park two-temperature relaxation, the finite-rate ionization network, and pointwise Navier–Stokes regime evaluators with their causal-effect wrappers. A stagnation line with a fitted shock runs entirely on these, with no grid.',
    caveat: null,
    evidence: [
      { text: 'MMS to machine ε', href: '/validation/#mms-tgv' },
      { text: 'Envelope placard example', href: '/examples/#flight-envelope-placard' },
    ],
  },
];

const precision = `/// Working precision.
pub type FloatType = f64; // or f32, or deep_causality_num::Float106`;
---
<section class="solvers page" data-rise>
  <header class="sec-head">
    <p class="eyebrow eyebrow-coord">
      <span class="ix">04 / 07</span><span class="lb">Multiple Solver Paradigms</span>
    </p>
    <h2>Multiple Solver Paradigms</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Calculus-based, compression-based and analytic. All three sit behind the
      same <code>CfdFlow</code> language and the same scalar type, so you pick
      the best fit for your problem: the DEC solver for an incompressible
      cavity, the QTT marcher for a reentry layer, a fitted closure for the
      stagnation line. Dispatch resolves at compile time from the config type.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="stack">
    {families.map((f, i) => (
      <article class="layer rise" style={`--i:${i}`}>
        <div class="layer-label">
          <span class="lk">{f.label}</span>
          <span class="lv">{f.key}</span>
        </div>
        <div class="layer-body">
          <h3>{f.title}</h3>
          <p class="for-what">{f.forWhat}</p>
          <p class="detail">{f.body}</p>
          {f.caveat && <p class="caveat">{f.caveat}</p>}
          <div class="ev-row">
            {f.evidence.map((e) => (
              <a class="chip chip-mono" href={e.href}>
                {e.text}<span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
              </a>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="precision">
    <h3>Precision as a Parameter</h3>
    <p class="detail">
      Every theory, solver, stage and observable is generic over one real
      scalar. A program fixes a single alias and the whole computation runs at
      that precision: <code>f32</code> for speed, <code>f64</code> for the
      industry-standard choice, or <code>Float106</code> for reference-grade
      results with up to 30 significant digits.
    </p>
    <Code code={precision} lang="rust" themes={SHIKI} />
    <p class="detail">
      Specification constants stay exact <code>f64</code> literals and are
      lifted into the working precision, so changing the alias reruns the whole
      program somewhere else on the ladder. That turns error attribution into
      an experiment. The plasma-blackout corridor was flown at <code>f64</code>
      and again at 106-bit: every gate and every discrete event step came out
      identical, the continuous witnesses agreed to 15 or 16 significant
      digits, and it cost about 11× the wall clock. Round-off is therefore not
      what limits that corridor.
    </p>
    <p class="caveat">
      The same experiment sets the floor. The corridor does not run at
      <code>f32</code> at all: <code>h²</code> in the then-flown Saha kernel is
      4.4e-67 and underflows the <code>f32</code> exponent range at step 1.
      Precision is a parameter, not a free one.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .solvers { max-width: var(--w-panel); }
  .sec-head { margin-bottom: var(--space-6); }
  .sec-head h2 { margin: var(--space-3) 0 var(--space-3); }
  .lede { color: var(--fg-1); max-width: var(--measure-lede); margin: 0; }

  .stack { border-top: 1px solid var(--line-1); }

  .layer {
    position: relative;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    padding: var(--space-5) var(--space-4);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-1);
    transition: background-color var(--dur-fast) var(--ease-out);
  }
  @media (min-width: 720px) {
    .layer { grid-template-columns: var(--w-label) 1fr; gap: var(--space-5); }
  }
  /* The tick grows 18px → 28px on hover (DESIGN.md §8.4). Implemented as
     scaleY rather than an animated `height`, which §13.10 bans: 28/18 = 1.556
     on a fixed 18px base gives the same result on the compositor. */
  .layer::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 50%;
    width: 3px;
    height: 18px;
    transform: translateY(-50%) scaleY(1);
    transform-origin: center;
    border-radius: 0 var(--radius-sm) var(--radius-sm) 0;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 55%, transparent);
    transition: transform var(--dur-med) var(--ease-out),
                background-color var(--dur-med) var(--ease-out);
  }
  .layer:hover { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 5%, transparent); }
  .layer:hover::before { transform: translateY(-50%) scaleY(1.556); background: var(--accent); }

  .layer-label { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-1); }
  .lk {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-eyebrow);
    letter-spacing: var(--ls-eyebrow);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--fg-2);
  }
  .lv {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-mono-sm);
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .layer-body h3 { margin: 0 0 var(--space-2); }
  .for-what { color: var(--fg-0); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); margin: 0 0 var(--space-3); }
  .detail { color: var(--fg-1); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); max-width: var(--measure); margin: 0 0 var(--space-4); }
  .caveat {
    color: var(--fg-2);
    font-size: var(--t-body-sm);
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-4);
    padding-left: var(--space-4);
    border-left: 1px solid var(--line-2);
  }
  .ev-row { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--space-2); }

  .precision { padding: var(--space-5) var(--space-4) 0; }
  .precision h3 { margin: 0 0 var(--space-3); font-size: var(--t-h4); }
</style>
